    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d488">

                <pb facs="0457" n="341" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg341"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 839.00/481.]</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the American Minister to the
                        Dominican Republic</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegrams.—Paraphrases.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-01-23">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 23, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The attention of the Haitian Government has been called to the report regarding
                    Desiderio Arias conveyed by your telegram of January 19. Press dispatches state
                    that the revolution is gaining <pb facs="0458" n="342" xml:id="pg342"/> ground,
                    under Arias near Monte Cristi and under Zenón Toribio near the Haitian frontier.
                    If the report and dispatches are confirmed, the Secretary of Legation or other
                    reliable messenger should be sent at once to Arias and Toribio<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d488-f1"/> to urge upon them the necessity of ceasing revolutionary
                    activity and of abiding the result of the coming elections; to point out that
                    the United States, due to its treaty relations, has a vital interest in the
                    peace of the Dominican Republic; and further that it is the practice of the
                    Government of the United States to refuse to recognize any Government resulting
                    from a revolution unless it appears to represent the will of the people and to
                    be able and willing to respond to its international obligations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Knox.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d488-f1" n="1" xml:id="d488-f1-content">
                <p>Zenón Toribio (or Torribio), one of the Morales group of insurrectionists; see
                        <ref target="frus1911#pg_171">For. Rel. 1911, pp. 171</ref> and <ref
                        target="frus1911#pg_174">174</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
